WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:0

00:13.876 --> 00:18.026 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

00:19.356 --> 00:20.356 align:middle
Good afternoon.

00:21.296 --> 00:23.856 align:middle
Before I get started,
I just want to say

00:23.856 --> 00:28.036 align:middle
that our thoughts are with those
who were injured this morning.

00:29.396 --> 00:32.246 align:middle
Today the Federal Open
Market Committee decided

00:32.246 --> 00:35.216 align:middle
to raise the target range
for the federal funds rate

00:35.566 --> 00:37.546 align:middle
by 1/4 percentage point,

00:38.066 --> 00:40.706 align:middle
bringing it to 1
to 1-1/4 percent.

00:41.386 --> 00:45.646 align:middle
Our decision to make another
gradual reduction in the amount

00:45.646 --> 00:50.386 align:middle
of policy accommodation reflects
the progress the economy has

00:50.386 --> 00:54.936 align:middle
made, and is expected to make,
toward maximum employment

00:55.236 --> 00:58.886 align:middle
and price stability objectives
assigned to us by law.

00:59.886 --> 01:02.386 align:middle
We also released
today, as an addendum

01:02.386 --> 01:06.486 align:middle
to our Policy Normalization
Principles and Plans,

01:06.846 --> 01:10.676 align:middle
additional information on the
process that we will follow

01:11.026 --> 01:15.106 align:middle
in normalizing the size of our
balance sheet once we determine

01:15.106 --> 01:17.596 align:middle
that it is appropriate
to begin doing so.

01:17.596 --> 01:21.246 align:middle
I'll have more to say about
our interest rate decision

01:21.576 --> 01:23.316 align:middle
and our balance sheet policy,

01:23.666 --> 01:26.676 align:middle
but first I'll review
recent economic developments

01:26.676 --> 01:27.416 align:middle
and the outlook.

01:28.516 --> 01:30.856 align:middle
Following a slowdown
in the first quarter,

01:31.196 --> 01:34.006 align:middle
economic growth appears
to have rebounded,

01:34.416 --> 01:38.466 align:middle
resulting in a moderate pace
of growth so far this year.

01:39.306 --> 01:43.696 align:middle
Household spending, which was
particularly soft earlier this

01:43.696 --> 01:47.086 align:middle
year, has been supported
by solid fundamentals,

01:47.466 --> 01:50.256 align:middle
including ongoing
improvement in the job market

01:50.706 --> 01:54.176 align:middle
and relatively high levels of
consumer sentiment and wealth.

01:55.276 --> 01:59.076 align:middle
Business investment, which was
weak for much of last year,

01:59.076 --> 02:00.796 align:middle
has continued to expand.

02:01.546 --> 02:04.166 align:middle
And exports have shown
greater strength this year,

02:04.506 --> 02:07.256 align:middle
in part reflecting a
pickup in global growth.

02:08.146 --> 02:12.566 align:middle
Overall, we continue to expect
that the economy will expand

02:12.826 --> 02:15.606 align:middle
at a moderate pace over
the next few years.

02:16.456 --> 02:19.206 align:middle
In the labor market,
job gains have averaged

02:19.206 --> 02:23.726 align:middle
about 160,000 per month
since the start of the year--

02:24.386 --> 02:27.966 align:middle
a solid rate of growth that,
although a little slower

02:27.966 --> 02:31.416 align:middle
than last year, remains
well above estimates

02:31.416 --> 02:35.826 align:middle
of the pace necessary to absorb
new entrants to the labor force.

02:36.846 --> 02:40.036 align:middle
The unemployment rate has fallen
about 1/2 percentage point

02:40.036 --> 02:44.516 align:middle
since the beginning of the year
and was 4.3 percent in May,

02:44.516 --> 02:47.546 align:middle
a low level by historical
standards

02:47.936 --> 02:53.466 align:middle
and modestly below the median
of FOMC participants' estimates

02:53.466 --> 02:55.296 align:middle
of its longer-run normal level.

02:56.286 --> 03:00.166 align:middle
Broader measures of labor market
utilization have also improved

03:00.226 --> 03:00.756 align:middle
this year.

03:01.706 --> 03:05.756 align:middle
Participation in the labor force
has been little changed, on net,

03:06.136 --> 03:07.676 align:middle
for about three years.

03:08.916 --> 03:11.096 align:middle
Given the underlying
downward trend

03:11.426 --> 03:15.296 align:middle
in participation stemming
largely from the aging

03:15.296 --> 03:16.856 align:middle
of the U.S. population,

03:17.486 --> 03:20.846 align:middle
a relatively steady
participation rate is a further

03:20.846 --> 03:24.136 align:middle
sign of improving conditions
in the labor market.

03:25.276 --> 03:27.256 align:middle
Looking ahead, we expect

03:27.256 --> 03:30.376 align:middle
that the job market will
strengthen somewhat further.

03:31.486 --> 03:34.826 align:middle
Turning to inflation,
the 12-month change

03:34.826 --> 03:39.836 align:middle
in the price index for personal
consumption expenditures was 1.7

03:39.836 --> 03:44.406 align:middle
percent in April, up from less
than 1 percent last summer

03:44.906 --> 03:47.626 align:middle
but down somewhat over
the past few months.

03:48.416 --> 03:51.766 align:middle
Core inflation-- which
excludes the volatile food

03:51.766 --> 03:55.766 align:middle
and energy categories and
tends to be a better indicator

03:55.766 --> 03:59.016 align:middle
of future inflation--
has also edged lower.

03:59.686 --> 04:01.676 align:middle
The recent lower readings

04:01.676 --> 04:06.786 align:middle
on inflation have been driven
significantly by what appear

04:06.786 --> 04:11.186 align:middle
to be one-off reductions in
certain categories of prices,

04:11.576 --> 04:15.916 align:middle
such as wireless telephone
services and prescription drugs.

04:16.726 --> 04:20.286 align:middle
These price declines will,
as a matter of arithmetic,

04:20.746 --> 04:23.596 align:middle
restrain the 12-month
inflation figures

04:23.916 --> 04:27.776 align:middle
until the extraordinarily
low March reading drops

04:27.776 --> 04:29.206 align:middle
out of the calculation.

04:30.176 --> 04:33.866 align:middle
However, with employment near
its maximum sustainable level

04:33.866 --> 04:36.906 align:middle
and the labor market
continuing to strengthen,

04:36.906 --> 04:40.336 align:middle
the Committee still
expects inflation to move up

04:40.636 --> 04:44.436 align:middle
and stabilize around 2 percent
over the next couple of years,

04:44.986 --> 04:47.186 align:middle
in line with our
longer-run objective.

04:48.136 --> 04:49.626 align:middle
Nonetheless, in light

04:49.626 --> 04:52.296 align:middle
of the softer recent
inflation readings,

04:52.616 --> 04:56.606 align:middle
the Committee is monitoring
inflation developments closely.

04:57.746 --> 05:00.606 align:middle
Let me now turn to the
economic projections

05:00.606 --> 05:03.386 align:middle
that Committee participants
submitted for this meeting.

05:04.426 --> 05:07.986 align:middle
As always, participants
conditioned their projections

05:07.986 --> 05:12.356 align:middle
on their own individual views
of appropriate monetary policy,

05:13.156 --> 05:16.726 align:middle
which, in turn, depend on
each participant's assessment

05:17.066 --> 05:19.616 align:middle
of the many factors
that shape the outlook.

05:20.516 --> 05:22.456 align:middle
The median projection for growth

05:22.456 --> 05:27.226 align:middle
of inflation-adjusted gross
domestic product, or real GDP,

05:27.546 --> 05:32.776 align:middle
is 2.2 percent this year and
edges down to 1.9 percent

05:33.126 --> 05:38.306 align:middle
by 2019, slightly above its
estimated longer-run rate.

05:39.606 --> 05:43.096 align:middle
The median projection for
the unemployment rate stands

05:43.096 --> 05:47.906 align:middle
at 4.3 percent in the fourth
quarter of this year and ticks

05:47.906 --> 05:52.346 align:middle
down to 4.2 percent
in 2018 and 2019,

05:53.026 --> 05:55.446 align:middle
modestly below the
median estimate

05:55.446 --> 05:57.256 align:middle
of its longer-run normal rate.

05:58.356 --> 06:02.766 align:middle
Finally, the median inflation
projection is 1.6 percent this

06:02.766 --> 06:07.806 align:middle
year and rises to 2
percent in 2018 and 2019.

06:08.786 --> 06:11.156 align:middle
Compared with the
projections made in March,

06:11.446 --> 06:14.016 align:middle
real GDP growth is
little changed,

06:14.496 --> 06:18.396 align:middle
the unemployment rate follows
a moderately lower path,

06:18.396 --> 06:21.046 align:middle
and inflation-- although
marked down this year

06:21.096 --> 06:23.046 align:middle
for reasons I mentioned
earlier--

06:23.556 --> 06:26.116 align:middle
is unchanged over the
following two years.

06:26.896 --> 06:28.896 align:middle
In addition, the median estimate

06:28.896 --> 06:31.966 align:middle
of the longer-run normal
unemployment rate moved

06:31.966 --> 06:34.506 align:middle
down a tenth to 4.6 percent.

06:35.676 --> 06:38.766 align:middle
Returning to monetary
policy, for the past year

06:38.766 --> 06:43.396 align:middle
and a half the FOMC has been
gradually increasing its target

06:43.396 --> 06:47.436 align:middle
range for the federal funds rate
as the economy has continued

06:47.466 --> 06:49.396 align:middle
to make progress
toward our goals

06:49.696 --> 06:52.186 align:middle
of maximum employment
and price stability.

06:53.106 --> 06:55.976 align:middle
Our decision today
continues this process.

06:56.786 --> 06:59.866 align:middle
We continue to expect
that the ongoing strength

06:59.866 --> 07:03.326 align:middle
of the economy will
warrant gradual increases

07:03.326 --> 07:07.476 align:middle
in the federal funds rate to
sustain a healthy labor market

07:07.856 --> 07:09.566 align:middle
and stabilize inflation

07:09.806 --> 07:12.426 align:middle
around our 2 percent
longer-run objective.

07:13.366 --> 07:14.776 align:middle
That's based on our view

07:14.776 --> 07:18.226 align:middle
that the federal funds rate
remains somewhat below its

07:18.226 --> 07:22.246 align:middle
neutral level-- that is, the
level of the federal funds rate

07:22.446 --> 07:25.726 align:middle
that is neither expansionary
nor contractionary

07:26.306 --> 07:29.406 align:middle
and keeps the economy
operating on an even keel.

07:30.886 --> 07:33.736 align:middle
Because the neutral rate
is currently quite low

07:33.736 --> 07:35.436 align:middle
by historical standards,

07:36.056 --> 07:38.586 align:middle
the federal funds rate
would not have to rise all

07:38.586 --> 07:42.306 align:middle
that much further to get
to a neutral policy stance.

07:43.086 --> 07:45.736 align:middle
But because we also
expect the neutral level

07:45.736 --> 07:49.396 align:middle
of the federal funds rate
to rise somewhat over time,

07:49.706 --> 07:53.696 align:middle
additional gradual rate hikes
are likely to be appropriate

07:53.696 --> 07:58.216 align:middle
over the next few years to
sustain the economic expansion.

07:59.256 --> 08:02.316 align:middle
Even so, the Committee
continues to anticipate

08:02.316 --> 08:04.086 align:middle
that the longer-run
neutral level

08:04.356 --> 08:08.656 align:middle
of the federal funds rate is
likely to remain below levels

08:08.656 --> 08:11.166 align:middle
that prevailed in
previous decades.

08:12.136 --> 08:15.656 align:middle
This view is consistent with
participants' projections

08:15.656 --> 08:18.036 align:middle
of appropriate monetary policy.

08:18.976 --> 08:22.616 align:middle
The median projection for
the federal funds rate is 1.4

08:22.616 --> 08:26.946 align:middle
percent at the end of this
year, 2.1 percent at the end

08:26.946 --> 08:32.886 align:middle
of next year, and 2.9 percent at
the end of 2019, about in line

08:32.886 --> 08:35.516 align:middle
with its estimated
longer-run value.

08:36.456 --> 08:40.406 align:middle
Compared with the projections
made in March, the median path

08:40.406 --> 08:43.816 align:middle
for the federal funds rate
is essentially unchanged.

08:44.836 --> 08:48.706 align:middle
As always, the economic
outlook is highly uncertain,

08:49.016 --> 08:51.676 align:middle
and participants will
adjust their assessments

08:51.926 --> 08:56.016 align:middle
of the appropriate path for the
federal funds rate in response

08:56.016 --> 08:59.366 align:middle
to changes to their
economic outlooks and views

08:59.366 --> 09:01.246 align:middle
of the risks to their outlooks.

09:01.866 --> 09:06.536 align:middle
As I've noted previously, policy
is not on a preset course.

09:07.596 --> 09:10.266 align:middle
Let me now turn to
our balance sheet.

09:10.876 --> 09:14.216 align:middle
As I noted in our policy
statement, we are continuing

09:14.216 --> 09:16.476 align:middle
to maintain the size
of our balance sheet

09:17.106 --> 09:20.956 align:middle
by reinvesting proceeds from
maturing Treasury securities

09:21.536 --> 09:23.996 align:middle
and principal payments
from agency debt

09:23.996 --> 09:25.776 align:middle
and mortgage-backed securities.

09:26.616 --> 09:29.436 align:middle
Provided that the
economy evolves broadly

09:29.436 --> 09:33.386 align:middle
as the Committee
anticipates, we currently expect

09:33.456 --> 09:37.866 align:middle
to begin implementing a balance
sheet normalization program

09:38.056 --> 09:38.716 align:middle
this year.

09:39.686 --> 09:44.046 align:middle
Consistent with the principles
and plans we released in 2014,

09:44.656 --> 09:48.426 align:middle
this program would gradually
decrease our reinvestments

09:48.876 --> 09:53.046 align:middle
and initiate a gradual and
largely predictable decline

09:53.216 --> 09:55.196 align:middle
in our securities holdings.

09:56.266 --> 09:59.746 align:middle
The addendum to our Policy
Normalization Principles

09:59.746 --> 10:04.166 align:middle
and Plans that we released today
provides further information.

10:05.026 --> 10:07.716 align:middle
For both Treasury and
agency securities,

10:08.206 --> 10:12.956 align:middle
we will reinvest proceeds from
our holdings only to the extent

10:12.956 --> 10:18.286 align:middle
that they exceed gradually
rising caps on the reductions

10:18.286 --> 10:19.826 align:middle
in our securities holdings.

10:20.666 --> 10:24.516 align:middle
Initially, these caps will be
set at relatively low levels--

10:25.146 --> 10:27.776 align:middle
$6 billion per month
for Treasuries

10:28.116 --> 10:31.056 align:middle
and $4 billion per
month for agencies.

10:31.316 --> 10:35.696 align:middle
So, any proceeds exceeding those
amounts would be reinvested.

10:36.636 --> 10:40.336 align:middle
These caps will gradually
rise over the course of a year

10:40.766 --> 10:44.696 align:middle
to maximums of $30 billion
per month for Treasuries

10:45.106 --> 10:48.696 align:middle
and $20 billion per month
for agency securities

10:49.276 --> 10:53.266 align:middle
and will remain in place through
the normalization process.

10:54.736 --> 10:57.506 align:middle
By limiting the volume
of securities

10:57.786 --> 11:00.396 align:middle
that private investors
will have to absorb

11:00.826 --> 11:04.696 align:middle
as we reduce our holdings,
the caps should guard

11:04.696 --> 11:07.276 align:middle
against outsized moves
in interest rates

11:07.436 --> 11:09.816 align:middle
and other potential
market strains.

11:10.906 --> 11:14.756 align:middle
As I previously noted, when
our securities holdings begin

11:14.756 --> 11:18.476 align:middle
to gradually decline,
so too will the supply

11:18.566 --> 11:21.466 align:middle
of reserve balances
in the banking system.

11:22.276 --> 11:25.776 align:middle
At some point, probably a
few years down the road,

11:26.246 --> 11:29.036 align:middle
the Committee will bring the
decline in our balance sheet

11:29.036 --> 11:32.936 align:middle
to an end as the quantity
of reserves is normalized.

11:33.646 --> 11:36.476 align:middle
I can't tell you what the
longer-run normal level

11:36.476 --> 11:40.806 align:middle
of reserve balances will
be because that will depend

11:40.806 --> 11:44.246 align:middle
on the Committee's
eventual decisions about how

11:44.246 --> 11:48.676 align:middle
to implement monetary policy
most efficiently and effectively

11:48.676 --> 11:52.156 align:middle
in the longer run,
as well as a number

11:52.156 --> 11:55.086 align:middle
of as-yet unknown elements,

11:55.086 --> 11:58.936 align:middle
including the banking system's
future demand for reserves

11:59.446 --> 12:02.726 align:middle
and various factors that
may affect the daily supply

12:02.726 --> 12:03.546 align:middle
of reserves.

12:04.276 --> 12:08.496 align:middle
What I can tell you is that
we anticipate reducing reserve

12:08.496 --> 12:11.516 align:middle
balances and our
overall balance sheet

12:11.786 --> 12:15.746 align:middle
to levels appreciably below
those seen in recent years

12:16.306 --> 12:19.126 align:middle
but larger than before
the financial crisis.

12:20.276 --> 12:24.036 align:middle
As readers of our minutes
know, the Committee has

12:24.036 --> 12:29.156 align:middle
on previous occasions discussed
potential long-run frameworks

12:29.356 --> 12:31.616 align:middle
for implementing
monetary policy.

12:32.636 --> 12:36.876 align:middle
Decisions about the appropriate
long-run framework do not need

12:36.876 --> 12:38.866 align:middle
to be made for quite some time,

12:39.526 --> 12:42.416 align:middle
and our future deliberations
will benefit

12:42.416 --> 12:45.196 align:middle
from the experience we
will gain during the

12:45.196 --> 12:46.916 align:middle
normalization process.

12:47.726 --> 12:49.876 align:middle
At this point, I'll just point

12:49.876 --> 12:52.456 align:middle
out that our current
system is working well

12:52.906 --> 12:54.986 align:middle
and has some important
advantages.

12:55.556 --> 12:59.056 align:middle
In particular, it is simple
and efficient to operate,

12:59.606 --> 13:02.536 align:middle
does not require active
management of the supply

13:02.536 --> 13:05.316 align:middle
of reserves, and,
most importantly,

13:05.586 --> 13:08.586 align:middle
provides good control over
the federal funds rate

13:08.936 --> 13:11.556 align:middle
and effective transmission
of changes

13:11.556 --> 13:15.106 align:middle
in the federal funds rate to
broader money market rates.

13:16.056 --> 13:19.046 align:middle
And because our current
system is likely compatible

13:19.046 --> 13:21.656 align:middle
with a much smaller
quantity of reserves,

13:22.156 --> 13:26.026 align:middle
our plan for gradually reducing
our balance sheet does not

13:26.156 --> 13:29.356 align:middle
constrain the Committee's
future options for how

13:29.356 --> 13:31.426 align:middle
to implement monetary policy.

13:32.206 --> 13:36.846 align:middle
Finally, as noted in today's
addendum, the Committee affirmed

13:37.206 --> 13:38.956 align:middle
that changing the target range

13:38.956 --> 13:42.006 align:middle
for the federal funds
rate is our primary means

13:42.006 --> 13:44.746 align:middle
of adjusting the stance
of monetary policy.

13:45.506 --> 13:48.926 align:middle
In other words, the balance
sheet is not intended

13:48.926 --> 13:53.996 align:middle
to be an active tool for
monetary policy in normal times.

13:54.596 --> 13:58.866 align:middle
However, the Committee would be
prepared to resume reinvestments

13:59.246 --> 14:03.446 align:middle
if a material deterioration
in the economic outlook were

14:03.446 --> 14:07.226 align:middle
to warrant a sizable reduction
in the federal funds rate.

14:07.896 --> 14:10.276 align:middle
More generally, the
Committee would be prepared

14:10.276 --> 14:15.286 align:middle
to use its full range of tools,
including altering the size

14:15.286 --> 14:17.336 align:middle
and composition of
its balance sheet,

14:17.936 --> 14:20.356 align:middle
if future economic
conditions were

14:20.356 --> 14:23.606 align:middle
to warrant a more
accommodative monetary policy

14:24.166 --> 14:28.316 align:middle
than can be achieved solely by
reducing the federal funds rate.

14:29.066 --> 14:29.756 align:middle
Thank you.

14:29.756 --> 14:31.856 align:middle
I'll now be happy to
take your questions.

14:32.986 --> 14:39.816 align:middle
NICK TIMIRAOS.

14:39.816 --> 14:42.706 align:middle
Nick Timiraos of the
Wall Street Journal.

14:42.706 --> 14:46.636 align:middle
Chair Yellen, the principles you
released today say the balance

14:46.636 --> 14:50.596 align:middle
sheet wind-down should commence
once interest rate normalization

14:51.186 --> 14:52.226 align:middle
is "well under way."

14:52.636 --> 14:54.256 align:middle
With this latest rate increase,

14:54.506 --> 14:58.226 align:middle
do you believe normalization
is now well under way?

14:59.836 --> 15:00.206 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

15:00.206 --> 15:02.876 align:middle
So that is something that
we've said for some time,

15:02.876 --> 15:06.546 align:middle
and I've previously-- when
I've been asked what "well

15:06.546 --> 15:11.846 align:middle
under way" means-- said
that I don't want to define

15:11.846 --> 15:14.626 align:middle
that in purely quantitative
terms

15:15.056 --> 15:17.666 align:middle
but rather in qualitative terms.

15:17.926 --> 15:22.416 align:middle
So there's no specific level
of the federal funds rate

15:22.726 --> 15:25.456 align:middle
that means we're well under way.

15:25.836 --> 15:30.216 align:middle
But it's also a question of
not only the current level,

15:30.216 --> 15:34.176 align:middle
but our confidence in the
outlook and our projections

15:34.246 --> 15:37.786 align:middle
for the future path of
the federal funds rate.

15:38.146 --> 15:42.386 align:middle
So we have increased our
federal funds rate target now

15:42.626 --> 15:43.766 align:middle
several times.

15:44.626 --> 15:51.156 align:middle
Our outlook is that we
anticipate further increases

15:52.386 --> 15:55.816 align:middle
this year and next year
for the federal funds rate,

15:56.166 --> 16:02.346 align:middle
and our statement indicates
that if the economy continues

16:02.346 --> 16:04.896 align:middle
to evolve in the
manner that we expect,

16:05.216 --> 16:08.236 align:middle
that we would feel the
conditions are in--

16:08.396 --> 16:12.546 align:middle
will be in place to begin
this process this year.

16:13.896 --> 16:15.016 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

16:15.016 --> 16:15.996 align:middle
Steve Liesman, CNBC.

16:16.026 --> 16:18.206 align:middle
Madam Chair, I'm wondering if
you've talked to the President

16:18.546 --> 16:21.236 align:middle
or members of his staff about
the possibility of staying

16:21.236 --> 16:23.056 align:middle
on as Chair for a second term.

16:23.546 --> 16:26.866 align:middle
I'm also wondering if you
would consider doing that--

16:26.866 --> 16:28.526 align:middle
that's something you
thought about doing.

16:29.266 --> 16:32.306 align:middle
And, finally, there are
three vacancies on the Fed.

16:32.306 --> 16:35.056 align:middle
Have you-- do you have any
comment at all for the President

16:35.056 --> 16:36.646 align:middle
about his failure
to nominate anybody

16:36.646 --> 16:37.536 align:middle
for those three positions?

16:37.536 --> 16:37.826 align:middle
Thank you.

16:37.866 --> 16:38.476 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

16:38.476 --> 16:42.326 align:middle
So what I've said about my own
situation is that I fully intend

16:42.326 --> 16:47.626 align:middle
to serve out my term as Chair,
which ends in early February.

16:48.126 --> 16:50.586 align:middle
I have not had conversations

16:50.586 --> 16:54.076 align:middle
with the President
about future plans.

16:54.366 --> 16:58.836 align:middle
And I do very much hope-- I know
that they have been working hard

16:58.886 --> 17:03.396 align:middle
to identify appropriate
nominees for the open slots,

17:03.776 --> 17:08.376 align:middle
and I do very much hope that
there will be nominations

17:08.376 --> 17:10.066 align:middle
in the not-too-distant future,

17:10.066 --> 17:13.516 align:middle
and that the Senate will
take those up expeditiously.

17:13.516 --> 17:16.136 align:middle
I look forward to
having a full Board.

17:16.186 --> 17:16.706 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

17:16.706 --> 17:20.166 align:middle
And, I'm sorry, just to follow
up: Do you desire to stay on?

17:20.166 --> 17:20.436 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

17:20.436 --> 17:22.506 align:middle
I really don't have anything
for you at this point.

17:23.106 --> 17:23.766 align:middle
SAM FLEMING.

17:26.626 --> 17:27.276 align:middle
Thanks very much.

17:27.396 --> 17:29.146 align:middle
Sam Fleming from
the Financial Times.

17:29.496 --> 17:31.556 align:middle
We've now had a very
long streak of--

17:31.556 --> 17:34.996 align:middle
or fairly long streak of
weak inflation numbers,

17:34.996 --> 17:37.866 align:middle
at least measured by the
CPI this morning as well.

17:38.366 --> 17:41.546 align:middle
Marketplace-based inflation
expectations are declining.

17:42.316 --> 17:44.646 align:middle
What kind of vigilance are
you now saying is needed

17:44.646 --> 17:46.186 align:middle
in terms of weak inflation?

17:46.556 --> 17:48.846 align:middle
How does that interact
with your policy outlook?

17:49.596 --> 17:51.636 align:middle
And would further
disappointments argue

17:51.686 --> 17:53.376 align:middle
for pressing "pause"
on rate hikes

17:53.436 --> 17:55.406 align:middle
or delaying balance
sheet runoff?

17:55.406 --> 17:58.236 align:middle
How do you think about those
two potential responses

17:58.236 --> 17:58.976 align:middle
to weak inflation?

17:59.706 --> 18:00.276 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

18:00.276 --> 18:03.216 align:middle
So let me just say, as I
emphasized in my statement

18:03.376 --> 18:08.356 align:middle
and always say, monetory policy
is not on a preset course.

18:08.686 --> 18:11.306 align:middle
We indicated in our
statement today

18:11.306 --> 18:15.486 align:middle
that we're closely monitoring
inflation developments

18:15.796 --> 18:19.316 align:middle
and certainly have
taken note of the fact

18:19.316 --> 18:22.786 align:middle
that there have been
several weak readings,

18:22.836 --> 18:24.956 align:middle
particularly on core inflation.

18:25.366 --> 18:29.166 align:middle
Our statement indicates
that we expect inflation

18:29.166 --> 18:31.916 align:middle
to remain low in the near term.

18:32.226 --> 18:34.756 align:middle
But, on the other hand,
we continue to feel

18:35.066 --> 18:38.316 align:middle
that with a strong labor market

18:38.316 --> 18:41.716 align:middle
and a labor market that's
continuing to strengthen,

18:42.226 --> 18:46.326 align:middle
the conditions are in place
for inflation to move up.

18:46.886 --> 18:50.826 align:middle
Now, obviously we need to
monitor that very carefully

18:52.176 --> 18:58.936 align:middle
and ensure, especially
with roughly five years

18:58.936 --> 19:02.086 align:middle
of inflation running under
our 2 percent objective--

19:02.556 --> 19:07.546 align:middle
that is a goal to which the
committee is strongly committed.

19:07.936 --> 19:12.496 align:middle
And we need to make sure that
we have in place the policies

19:12.616 --> 19:16.116 align:middle
that are necessary to
achieve 2 percent inflation,

19:16.426 --> 19:18.936 align:middle
and I pledge that
we will do that.

19:19.176 --> 19:22.716 align:middle
But let me say, with
respect to recent readings,

19:22.936 --> 19:28.166 align:middle
it's important not to
overreact to a few readings,

19:28.476 --> 19:31.806 align:middle
and data on inflation
can be noisy.

19:32.226 --> 19:38.336 align:middle
As I pointed out, there have
been some idiosyncratic factors

19:38.586 --> 19:42.166 align:middle
I think that have held down
inflation in recent months,

19:42.396 --> 19:45.936 align:middle
particularly a huge decline

19:45.936 --> 19:51.966 align:middle
in cell telephone service
plan prices, some declines

19:51.966 --> 19:53.596 align:middle
in prescription drugs.

19:53.876 --> 19:59.206 align:middle
We had an exceptionally low
reading on core PCE in March,

19:59.496 --> 20:05.336 align:middle
and that will continue to
hold down 12-month changes

20:05.556 --> 20:07.956 align:middle
until that reading drops out.

20:08.246 --> 20:12.276 align:middle
But we are-- this
morning's reading

20:12.276 --> 20:17.806 align:middle
on the CPI showed weakness
in a number of categories,

20:18.116 --> 20:19.616 align:middle
and it's certainly something

20:19.616 --> 20:24.346 align:middle
that we will be closely
monitoring in the months ahead.

20:24.786 --> 20:29.706 align:middle
We will-- we're focused on
making our policy decisions

20:29.886 --> 20:34.776 align:middle
on the medium-term outlook,
and we will, you know,

20:34.776 --> 20:38.836 align:middle
be looking carefully at
incoming data and, as always,

20:38.836 --> 20:42.706 align:middle
revising our outlook and
policy plans as appropriate.

20:43.656 --> 20:44.336 align:middle
SAM FLEMING.

20:44.516 --> 20:49.416 align:middle
Any implications for beginning
of runoff and further increases

20:49.416 --> 20:50.406 align:middle
in the fed funds rate?

20:50.406 --> 20:50.706 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

20:50.706 --> 20:55.016 align:middle
So-- continue, as
today's actions show,

20:55.436 --> 20:58.026 align:middle
to feel that the
economy is doing well,

20:58.296 --> 20:59.936 align:middle
is showing resilience.

21:00.406 --> 21:03.186 align:middle
We have a very strong
labor market,

21:03.186 --> 21:06.506 align:middle
an unemployment rate
that's declined

21:06.556 --> 21:09.526 align:middle
to levels we have
not seen since 2001.

21:10.066 --> 21:14.546 align:middle
And, even with some moderation
in the pace of job growth,

21:14.976 --> 21:18.156 align:middle
we have a labor market that
continues to strengthen

21:18.436 --> 21:23.256 align:middle
and policy remains--
remains accommodative.

21:23.656 --> 21:29.366 align:middle
So, I-- it's important
that inflation move

21:29.366 --> 21:31.426 align:middle
up to our 2 percent objective,

21:31.756 --> 21:35.966 align:middle
as our projections show
we continue to expect that

21:36.126 --> 21:38.396 align:middle
and believe the conditions
are in place.

21:38.876 --> 21:43.026 align:middle
But we will monitor
incoming data, obviously,

21:43.386 --> 21:47.056 align:middle
and be attentive to
rethinking our outlook

21:47.056 --> 21:48.356 align:middle
if it seems appropriate.

21:50.306 --> 21:52.556 align:middle
CRAIG TORRES.

21:54.666 --> 21:56.156 align:middle
Craig Torres from
Bloomberg News.

21:56.716 --> 22:00.816 align:middle
Hate to belabor the point on
inflation, but I was wondering,

22:01.556 --> 22:05.916 align:middle
I hear a lot of the so-called
NAIRU in your conversation now

22:05.916 --> 22:07.216 align:middle
and in other committee members'.

22:07.816 --> 22:09.856 align:middle
It's an unobservable thing.

22:10.026 --> 22:11.206 align:middle
At best, it's an estimate.

22:11.466 --> 22:13.696 align:middle
And the assumptions
in there seem to me

22:13.696 --> 22:17.686 align:middle
that the economy today is much
like the economy yesterday,

22:18.166 --> 22:19.436 align:middle
when, if anything, we've learned

22:19.436 --> 22:22.806 align:middle
that the post-recession
economy is vastly different

22:22.896 --> 22:24.616 align:middle
than it was before
the recession.

22:25.216 --> 22:28.026 align:middle
So I'm wondering, something
you've talked about is

22:28.026 --> 22:32.846 align:middle
to focus more on the change in
inflation-- actual inflation,

22:32.846 --> 22:34.626 align:middle
and is it going up
or is it going down,

22:34.626 --> 22:36.206 align:middle
and basing policy more on that.

22:36.686 --> 22:39.956 align:middle
What would be the risk of
that, and why not adopt

22:39.956 --> 22:43.556 align:middle
that if you have such a long
period of underperformance?

22:43.556 --> 22:43.906 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

22:43.906 --> 22:47.076 align:middle
Well, we are closely looking
at the actual performance

22:47.076 --> 22:51.976 align:middle
of inflation and altering
our views on the basis

22:51.976 --> 22:56.076 align:middle
of discrepancies between what
we see and our expectations.

22:56.416 --> 22:59.606 align:middle
And, well, it is
very difficult to pin

22:59.606 --> 23:03.406 align:middle
down what is the longer-run
normal rate of unemployment,

23:03.816 --> 23:06.996 align:middle
and there's a great deal
of uncertainty about it,

23:06.996 --> 23:10.386 align:middle
and it's hard to pin down,
especially given the fact

23:10.646 --> 23:14.236 align:middle
that the so-called Phillips
curve appears to be quite flat.

23:14.236 --> 23:18.236 align:middle
That means that inflation
doesn't respond very much

23:18.236 --> 23:21.146 align:middle
or very quickly to
movements in unemployment.

23:21.446 --> 23:26.316 align:middle
Nevertheless, that relationship,
I believe, remains at work.

23:26.646 --> 23:29.176 align:middle
We have seen that
operate historically.

23:29.676 --> 23:33.976 align:middle
Now, in the face of very low
unemployment that we have seen,

23:34.246 --> 23:38.866 align:middle
while wage growth has picked
up somewhat, it remains low,

23:39.216 --> 23:43.546 align:middle
and inflation is influenced by
a number of different factors,

23:43.746 --> 23:46.196 align:middle
but we certainly
haven't seen much

23:46.396 --> 23:49.486 align:middle
or any evident upward
pressure on inflation.

23:49.846 --> 23:54.436 align:middle
In light of that, the
Committee has successively moved

23:54.496 --> 23:59.276 align:middle
down its estimate of the normal
longer-run rate of unemployment,

23:59.526 --> 24:05.796 align:middle
and in this projection, it's
moved down to 4.6 percent,

24:05.796 --> 24:08.496 align:middle
a tenth lower than
it was last time.

24:08.496 --> 24:11.776 align:middle
So, while the unemployment
rate is below that,

24:12.636 --> 24:15.676 align:middle
it's not that much-- it's
not that much below it.

24:16.156 --> 24:17.476 align:middle
ANA SWANSON.

24:17.976 --> 24:19.786 align:middle
Ana Swanson, the
Washington Post.

24:20.006 --> 24:22.456 align:middle
We saw measures of consumer
and business confidence rise

24:22.456 --> 24:24.866 align:middle
after inauguration
on expectations

24:24.866 --> 24:26.546 align:middle
that the Administration
would move quickly

24:26.546 --> 24:29.256 align:middle
to introduce policy
changes like tax cuts

24:29.256 --> 24:30.516 align:middle
and infrastructure spending.

24:30.836 --> 24:33.726 align:middle
But some of those policy changes
have been slower to materialize

24:33.806 --> 24:35.076 align:middle
than initially expected.

24:35.426 --> 24:37.696 align:middle
How do you view the
positive and negative risks

24:37.756 --> 24:39.186 align:middle
from policy changes
to your outlook,

24:39.186 --> 24:41.736 align:middle
and has your view
changed on that at all

24:41.926 --> 24:45.456 align:middle
in the last six months?

24:45.846 --> 24:46.856 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

24:46.856 --> 24:48.956 align:middle
So I would say that business

24:49.296 --> 24:54.016 align:middle
and household sentiment
remains quite strong,

24:54.016 --> 24:59.566 align:middle
although many forecasters have
pushed back somewhat the timing

24:59.566 --> 25:04.546 align:middle
of the expected policy changes,
such as changes to tax policy

25:04.846 --> 25:07.146 align:middle
or fiscal policy more generally.

25:07.666 --> 25:12.376 align:middle
I would say that,
based on my observation

25:12.376 --> 25:16.596 align:middle
of actual spending
behavior and my discussions

25:16.596 --> 25:19.686 align:middle
with our wide range of contacts,

25:20.116 --> 25:27.076 align:middle
that I haven't seen very
much evidence that, thus far,

25:27.456 --> 25:33.096 align:middle
expectations of policy changes
have driven substantial changes

25:33.386 --> 25:37.466 align:middle
in either consumer spending
or investment spending.

25:37.936 --> 25:42.036 align:middle
So I really wouldn't expect
any significant pullback.

25:42.456 --> 25:44.276 align:middle
Many of our business contacts--

25:44.556 --> 25:47.636 align:middle
I think their confidence
remains high.

25:47.636 --> 25:50.706 align:middle
They've not really
changed their plans yet,

25:50.706 --> 25:53.196 align:middle
and they have a wait-and-see
attitude.

25:54.836 --> 26:00.186 align:middle
BINYAMIN APPELBAUM.

26:01.106 --> 26:03.306 align:middle
Binya Appelbaum,
the New York Times.

26:03.306 --> 26:05.346 align:middle
Measures of financial
conditions show

26:05.346 --> 26:08.736 align:middle
that since the Fed started
raising interest rates two years

26:08.736 --> 26:12.356 align:middle
ago, financial conditions
actually have loosened.

26:12.356 --> 26:15.076 align:middle
Consumer business borrowing
costs in many cases are down.

26:15.076 --> 26:17.646 align:middle
Do you have the sense that the
market is not listening to you?

26:17.826 --> 26:19.946 align:middle
How much of a concern
is that for you?

26:19.946 --> 26:22.176 align:middle
And, at some point, does it
convince you that you need

26:22.176 --> 26:23.976 align:middle
to raise rates perhaps
more quickly?

26:24.076 --> 26:24.416 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

26:24.796 --> 26:28.076 align:middle
Well, in deciding what the
appropriate path of rates is,

26:28.516 --> 26:32.086 align:middle
we take many different
factors into account.

26:32.596 --> 26:36.476 align:middle
We have certainly noticed
the stock market is

26:37.016 --> 26:39.416 align:middle
up considerably over
the last year.

26:39.766 --> 26:43.826 align:middle
That usually shows up in
financial conditions indexes

26:44.136 --> 26:47.456 align:middle
and is an important
reason why some

26:47.456 --> 26:51.066 align:middle
of them show easier
financial conditions.

26:51.066 --> 26:55.946 align:middle
There's been a modest
decrease recently in the value

26:55.946 --> 27:00.796 align:middle
of the dollar, although it's up
substantially since mid-2014.

27:00.796 --> 27:05.516 align:middle
So we take those
factors into account

27:05.836 --> 27:08.106 align:middle
in deriving our forecasts

27:08.106 --> 27:12.896 align:middle
and deciding the
appropriate stance of policy.

27:13.176 --> 27:15.476 align:middle
We have done that, and--

27:15.726 --> 27:20.156 align:middle
but other things also
affect the stance of policy.

27:20.466 --> 27:24.236 align:middle
So there really can't be
any simple relationship.

27:24.236 --> 27:27.136 align:middle
We're not targeting
financial conditions.

27:27.396 --> 27:30.546 align:middle
We're trying to set a path
of the federal funds rate,

27:30.726 --> 27:33.596 align:middle
but taking account of
those factors and others

27:33.806 --> 27:37.146 align:middle
that don't show up in a
financial conditions index.

27:37.406 --> 27:41.396 align:middle
We're trying to generate paths
for employment and inflation

27:41.676 --> 27:43.606 align:middle
that meet our mandated
objectives.

27:45.086 --> 27:45.736 align:middle
HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

27:46.766 --> 27:48.016 align:middle
Howard Schneider with Reuters.

27:48.386 --> 27:51.156 align:middle
So, on inflation again, I just
wonder, what's the possibility

27:51.156 --> 27:54.626 align:middle
that something more, sort of-- I
mean, nefarious is at work here,

27:54.676 --> 27:56.076 align:middle
right, which is that
the, sort of,

27:56.076 --> 27:58.846 align:middle
weight of central
bank credibility now--

27:58.896 --> 28:00.176 align:middle
for a generation, really--

28:00.906 --> 28:03.066 align:middle
plus globalization has
just pushed the world

28:03.066 --> 28:06.546 align:middle
into a low-inflation environment
that's going to be very hard

28:06.606 --> 28:08.406 align:middle
to get wages and
prices moving again.

28:09.176 --> 28:12.826 align:middle
And then, related to that,
you know, if NAIRU is 4.2,

28:12.826 --> 28:14.586 align:middle
why not 4 or why not 3.8?

28:14.656 --> 28:17.646 align:middle
You banked a quarter-- you know,
a full percentage point now,

28:17.786 --> 28:19.446 align:middle
so you're probably at less risk

28:19.446 --> 28:21.286 align:middle
of falling behind
whatever curve exists.

28:22.356 --> 28:25.546 align:middle
Why rush? CHAIR YELLEN.

28:25.986 --> 28:29.256 align:middle
So I don't think central
bank credibility--

28:29.256 --> 28:32.256 align:middle
at least the Fed's
credibility-- has been impaired.

28:32.936 --> 28:35.726 align:middle
We look at a whole
variety of indicators

28:35.726 --> 28:38.186 align:middle
of inflation expectations.

28:39.406 --> 28:42.496 align:middle
Professional forecasters--
whether it's in the Blue Chip

28:42.496 --> 28:45.486 align:middle
or the Survey of
Professional Forecasters,

28:45.796 --> 28:50.046 align:middle
those expectations have
remained quite steady

28:50.346 --> 28:55.066 align:middle
and in close alignment with
our 2 percent inflation target.

28:57.156 --> 29:03.176 align:middle
TIPS-based measures of inflation
compensation do not provide

29:03.176 --> 29:07.766 align:middle
straight reads on market
participants' estimates

29:08.036 --> 29:10.496 align:middle
and expectations
about inflation.

29:10.716 --> 29:12.956 align:middle
They embody other elements--

29:12.996 --> 29:16.656 align:middle
risk premia and liquidity
premia as well.

29:17.136 --> 29:21.156 align:middle
They had moved down
and now have moved--

29:21.266 --> 29:25.246 align:middle
they remain at low levels, but
they have moved back up again.

29:25.566 --> 29:28.476 align:middle
It is true that some
household surveys

29:28.476 --> 29:32.606 align:middle
of inflation expectations
have moved down,

29:32.636 --> 29:37.736 align:middle
but overall I wouldn't say that
we've seen a broad undermining

29:37.736 --> 29:40.176 align:middle
of inflation expectations.

29:40.556 --> 29:46.346 align:middle
But you asked also, I guess,

29:46.346 --> 29:51.926 align:middle
about have structural changes
perhaps impacted the inflation

29:51.926 --> 29:55.046 align:middle
process, and that
certainly is possible.

29:55.506 --> 30:00.436 align:middle
And estimates of the normal
longer-run unemployment rate--

30:00.746 --> 30:02.366 align:middle
they are quite uncertain.

30:02.726 --> 30:05.416 align:middle
I agree with your
assessment there.

30:05.416 --> 30:07.436 align:middle
We're really not
certain what they are.

30:07.896 --> 30:10.296 align:middle
And policy is not being--

30:10.296 --> 30:15.506 align:middle
is not based on some firmly
held preconceived notion.

30:15.746 --> 30:22.126 align:middle
We're watching very carefully
how the actual economy performs.

30:22.586 --> 30:27.776 align:middle
And, you know, I continue
to believe, though,

30:28.176 --> 30:34.726 align:middle
that with job growth
running well in excess,

30:35.066 --> 30:40.536 align:middle
even with the moderation of the
level that's needed to provide

30:40.616 --> 30:42.676 align:middle
for new entrants in
the labor market,

30:43.206 --> 30:46.136 align:middle
we do have a strengthening
economy.

30:47.726 --> 30:52.056 align:middle
With policy accommodative,
all that we're doing

30:52.056 --> 30:54.156 align:middle
in raising rates is moving--

30:54.446 --> 30:59.286 align:middle
removing a bit of accommodation
heading toward a neutral pace.

30:59.776 --> 31:02.676 align:middle
And I see that as appropriate.

31:02.766 --> 31:08.166 align:middle
We're not moving so
aggressively as to put a brake

31:08.166 --> 31:11.566 align:middle
on continued improvement
in the labor market.

31:11.946 --> 31:16.296 align:middle
But I think that
that's a prudent move,

31:16.436 --> 31:19.966 align:middle
to move in a gradual way
to remove accommodation,

31:20.276 --> 31:23.646 align:middle
with unemployment now--
and not only, I should say,

31:23.646 --> 31:27.176 align:middle
the unemployment rate,
but I think any indicator

31:27.176 --> 31:29.736 align:middle
of labor market performance
and tightness

31:30.136 --> 31:34.176 align:middle
that you could look at, whether
it's household perceptions

31:34.176 --> 31:39.086 align:middle
of the availability of jobs,
difficulty that firms report

31:39.086 --> 31:41.756 align:middle
in hiring workers, the rate

31:41.756 --> 31:44.776 align:middle
at which workers are
quitting their jobs,

31:45.086 --> 31:47.356 align:middle
the rate of job openings,

31:47.846 --> 31:52.926 align:middle
all of these indicators do
signal a tight labor market.

31:52.926 --> 31:55.096 align:middle
Now, with inflation
below 2 percent,

31:55.306 --> 31:58.736 align:middle
I think it's appropriate that
the labor market be that tight.

31:59.096 --> 32:03.206 align:middle
But, on the other hand, I think
we want to avoid the risk.

32:03.306 --> 32:07.126 align:middle
We want to keep the expansion
on a sustainable path

32:07.456 --> 32:11.486 align:middle
and avoid the risk that
at some point we need--

32:11.486 --> 32:15.906 align:middle
we find ourselves in a situation
where we've done nothing

32:16.156 --> 32:20.106 align:middle
and then need to raise
the funds rate so rapidly

32:20.326 --> 32:22.766 align:middle
that we risk a recession.

32:23.006 --> 32:26.126 align:middle
So moving to some
extent in a timely way

32:26.126 --> 32:29.506 align:middle
to remove accommodation
with a strong economy

32:29.706 --> 32:33.356 align:middle
and continued labor
market strength,

32:33.816 --> 32:38.066 align:middle
the Committee believes, is an
appropriate management of risks.

32:38.426 --> 32:41.146 align:middle
But we are attentive to the fact

32:41.486 --> 32:44.926 align:middle
that inflation is running
below our 2 percent objective,

32:45.356 --> 32:48.946 align:middle
that we've faced that
situation now for a long time,

32:49.376 --> 32:54.716 align:middle
and it's really quite essential
that we put in place policies

32:54.716 --> 32:57.106 align:middle
that will succeed in
moving inflation back

32:57.416 --> 33:00.996 align:middle
to our 2 percent objective,
and it's a symmetric objective.

33:00.996 --> 33:06.656 align:middle
So that's a risk
that we face as well.

33:06.656 --> 33:10.046 align:middle
The Committee believes we
have conditions in place

33:10.386 --> 33:13.626 align:middle
for inflation to move up,
but that's also a risk.

33:13.906 --> 33:17.166 align:middle
And those things point, I
think, to a gradual pace

33:17.166 --> 33:18.966 align:middle
of reducing accommodation.

33:20.096 --> 33:20.976 align:middle
NANCY MARSHALL-GENZER.

33:25.976 --> 33:28.226 align:middle
Hi, Nancy Marshall-Genzer
from Marketplace.

33:29.216 --> 33:32.476 align:middle
Recently, a group of economists
sent the Fed a letter earlier

33:32.476 --> 33:35.866 align:middle
this month disagreeing with
your 2 percent inflation target

33:35.866 --> 33:38.786 align:middle
and saying they would like the
economy to run a bit hotter.

33:38.786 --> 33:40.926 align:middle
They don't think the
labor market is so tight.

33:41.396 --> 33:44.226 align:middle
You say you're committed
to the 2 percent target,

33:44.456 --> 33:45.766 align:middle
but what do you say to them?

33:46.856 --> 33:47.416 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

33:47.726 --> 33:52.096 align:middle
So, at the time that we
adopted the 2 percent target--

33:52.096 --> 33:56.516 align:middle
it was back in 2012-- we had
a very thorough discussion

33:56.756 --> 34:00.936 align:middle
of the factors that should
determine what our inflation

34:00.936 --> 34:02.146 align:middle
objective should be.

34:02.566 --> 34:07.836 align:middle
And, you know, I believe that
was a well-thought-out decision.

34:07.836 --> 34:11.906 align:middle
Now, at the moment, we are
highly focused on trying

34:11.906 --> 34:14.436 align:middle
to achieve our 2
percent objective.

34:14.766 --> 34:17.036 align:middle
And we recognize the fact

34:17.036 --> 34:21.106 align:middle
that inflation has been running
below, and it's essential for us

34:21.146 --> 34:24.056 align:middle
to move inflation back
to that objective.

34:24.766 --> 34:29.906 align:middle
Now, we've learned a lot in
the meantime, and assessments

34:29.906 --> 34:34.496 align:middle
of the level of the neutral
likely level, currently

34:34.496 --> 34:35.666 align:middle
and going forward,

34:36.086 --> 34:39.406 align:middle
of the neutral federal
funds rate have changed

34:39.736 --> 34:42.996 align:middle
and are quite a bit
lower than they stood

34:42.996 --> 34:45.586 align:middle
in 2012 or earlier years.

34:46.076 --> 34:49.786 align:middle
And that means that
the economy is--

34:50.166 --> 34:55.066 align:middle
has the potential where
policy could be constrained

34:55.066 --> 34:59.416 align:middle
by the zero lower bound more
frequently than at the time

34:59.416 --> 35:02.496 align:middle
that we adopted our
2 percent objective.

35:02.916 --> 35:08.716 align:middle
So it's that recognition
that causes people

35:08.906 --> 35:11.436 align:middle
to think we might be better off

35:11.436 --> 35:13.396 align:middle
with a higher inflation
objective,

35:14.876 --> 35:16.866 align:middle
and that's an important set--

35:16.926 --> 35:19.446 align:middle
this is one of our
most critical decisions

35:19.746 --> 35:24.516 align:middle
and one we are attentive to
evidence and outside thinking.

35:24.516 --> 35:27.906 align:middle
It's one that we
will be reconsidering

35:27.906 --> 35:29.316 align:middle
at some future time.

35:29.856 --> 35:35.946 align:middle
And it's important for our
decisions to be informed

35:35.946 --> 35:39.006 align:middle
by a wide range of
views and research

35:39.236 --> 35:42.756 align:middle
which is ongoing inside
and outside the Fed.

35:43.256 --> 35:47.336 align:middle
But a reconsideration
of that objective needs

35:47.336 --> 35:51.436 align:middle
to take account not only of
benefits of a higher in--

35:51.486 --> 35:55.186 align:middle
potential benefits of a
higher inflation target,

35:55.426 --> 35:57.646 align:middle
but also the potential costs

35:57.726 --> 35:59.766 align:middle
that could be associated
with it.

35:59.996 --> 36:02.116 align:middle
It needs to be a
balanced assessment.

36:02.496 --> 36:05.266 align:middle
But I would say that this is one

36:05.266 --> 36:10.026 align:middle
of the most important
questions facing monetary policy

36:10.026 --> 36:15.086 align:middle
around the world in the future,
and we very much look forward

36:15.086 --> 36:19.046 align:middle
to seeing research by economists

36:19.126 --> 36:22.546 align:middle
that will help inform our
future decisions on this.

36:23.786 --> 36:27.196 align:middle
DON LEE. Thank you.

36:27.326 --> 36:28.666 align:middle
Don Lee with the L.A. Times.

36:29.206 --> 36:31.716 align:middle
The Fed's projections continue

36:31.716 --> 36:35.906 align:middle
to show the longer-run federal
funds rate at 3 percent.

36:36.086 --> 36:38.616 align:middle
The markets are expecting
2 percent.

36:39.146 --> 36:43.526 align:middle
How big of a concern is
that gap in your mind?

36:44.256 --> 36:46.796 align:middle
What are the reasons
for the disconnect?

36:46.796 --> 36:49.886 align:middle
And, you know, what
are the implications

36:49.886 --> 36:51.406 align:middle
and the risks for
the real economy?

36:51.406 --> 36:51.956 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

36:52.826 --> 36:57.146 align:middle
So let me first say,
it's not straightforward

36:57.186 --> 37:01.056 align:middle
to determine exactly what
expectations are embodied

37:01.416 --> 37:04.936 align:middle
in market prices because
there are term premia

37:05.386 --> 37:11.196 align:middle
that affect these rates, and
they may not really be as low

37:11.196 --> 37:14.186 align:middle
as one would infer
from a straight read.

37:14.736 --> 37:24.286 align:middle
That said, in part,
expectations reflect estimates

37:24.516 --> 37:28.486 align:middle
of what the neutral
federal funds rate is

37:28.816 --> 37:31.896 align:middle
and how it's likely
to evolve over time.

37:32.286 --> 37:35.516 align:middle
And views have changed
about that over time,

37:35.696 --> 37:38.506 align:middle
and there is a good deal
of uncertainty about it.

37:38.896 --> 37:44.376 align:middle
So we have recently
put out charts that try

37:44.376 --> 37:49.246 align:middle
to show what the range of
uncertainty is around our path

37:49.246 --> 37:51.196 align:middle
for the federal funds rate,

37:51.676 --> 37:53.836 align:middle
especially as one
goes further out.

37:54.106 --> 37:56.876 align:middle
There's a good deal
of uncertainty and--

37:57.076 --> 37:58.576 align:middle
that reflects the, like--

37:58.576 --> 38:02.026 align:middle
all the shocks that can affect
the evolution of the economy,

38:02.296 --> 38:05.606 align:middle
and also the fact, we're
quite uncertain ourselves

38:05.606 --> 38:09.906 align:middle
about what the likely--
what the evolution will be

38:09.906 --> 38:11.786 align:middle
of the neutral federal
funds rate.

38:12.166 --> 38:16.266 align:middle
So we do try to write that
down and provide the public

38:16.266 --> 38:20.166 align:middle
with information about
our current expectations.

38:20.466 --> 38:24.676 align:middle
And the median now stands
at around 3 percent,

38:25.106 --> 38:27.496 align:middle
but we have uncertainty
about that.

38:27.726 --> 38:29.566 align:middle
Many of my colleagues
and I think

38:29.616 --> 38:32.746 align:middle
that the current neutral
level is lower than that.

38:33.036 --> 38:35.276 align:middle
And as I said in my
opening statement,

38:35.596 --> 38:39.116 align:middle
the fed funds rate path
reflects an expectation

38:39.406 --> 38:43.746 align:middle
that that neutral rate will be
moving up some in future years.

38:44.036 --> 38:47.656 align:middle
But that remains uncertain,
and I think that's something

38:47.656 --> 38:50.676 align:middle
that market participants
are trying to assess,

38:50.916 --> 38:54.026 align:middle
and we will be as well.

38:54.776 --> 39:01.526 align:middle
DON LEE. But do you see greater
volatility in the markets

39:01.666 --> 39:05.796 align:middle
as it adjusts to the
difference in the gap

39:06.296 --> 39:09.566 align:middle
between the expectation and
what the Fed's projections are

39:09.566 --> 39:13.256 align:middle
for the longer-run
federal funds rate?

39:13.386 --> 39:13.816 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

39:13.816 --> 39:16.886 align:middle
Well, our expectations
are little changed.

39:16.886 --> 39:19.426 align:middle
I mean, the projections we
released today are essentially

39:19.626 --> 39:20.286 align:middle
identical to those.

39:20.286 --> 39:23.246 align:middle
And I think the market is aware
of the views of participants

39:23.346 --> 39:27.236 align:middle
and is assessing evidence
itself to form their own views.

39:27.236 --> 39:31.136 align:middle
And it's important that the
market take an independent look,

39:31.506 --> 39:34.936 align:middle
and that we get to understand

39:34.936 --> 39:39.736 align:middle
and see how market participants
are interpreting evidence

39:39.736 --> 39:41.626 align:middle
on the evolution of the economy.

39:41.626 --> 39:46.716 align:middle
So it's not an unhealthy
thing to have such a gap.

39:46.716 --> 39:50.916 align:middle
And, as I just said, our own
views are not set in stone

39:50.916 --> 39:53.846 align:middle
but are likely to
evolve as well over time.

39:53.876 --> 39:56.536 align:middle
MARTIN CRUTSINGER.

39:59.166 --> 40:00.846 align:middle
Marty Crutsinger,
Associated Press.

40:01.196 --> 40:05.316 align:middle
Back in 2013, when you-- the
Fed first raised the possibility

40:05.506 --> 40:08.836 align:middle
of trimming its bond
purchases, it created a bit

40:08.836 --> 40:10.756 align:middle
of turbulence in
financial markets.

40:10.756 --> 40:13.046 align:middle
The taper tantrum caused you

40:13.186 --> 40:17.506 align:middle
to reassess your
communication of that process.

40:17.926 --> 40:23.116 align:middle
This trimming of the
bond holdings, though--

40:23.116 --> 40:27.806 align:middle
the financial markets seem to
be taking that in a better way.

40:28.516 --> 40:32.506 align:middle
If down the road, though,
you see that there is more

40:32.506 --> 40:37.926 align:middle
of an adverse reaction, are you
planning to make any changes,

40:38.236 --> 40:40.916 align:middle
perhaps change the
caps to $30 billion,

40:40.916 --> 40:42.286 align:middle
in the $20 billion caps?

40:43.006 --> 40:46.706 align:middle
How do you assess how you'll
handle that going forward?

40:47.076 --> 40:47.356 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

40:48.576 --> 40:54.776 align:middle
So we have indicated for quite
some time, I guess, since our--

40:54.966 --> 40:59.436 align:middle
at least since our 2014
normalization principles,

40:59.866 --> 41:03.326 align:middle
that we wanted to
reduce our balance sheet

41:03.546 --> 41:06.136 align:middle
in a gradual and
predictable way.

41:06.556 --> 41:11.736 align:middle
And we have tried systematically
to communicate more

41:11.736 --> 41:15.836 align:middle
about how we would do that
as our plans have evolved.

41:16.226 --> 41:19.946 align:middle
And today's announcement
is another step

41:20.206 --> 41:26.646 align:middle
in providing further details
about how we plan to proceed

41:27.046 --> 41:31.446 align:middle
so that when this plan does go
into effect, no one is taken

41:31.446 --> 41:35.926 align:middle
by surprise and market
participants understand how this

41:35.926 --> 41:36.656 align:middle
will work.

41:37.086 --> 41:44.436 align:middle
I think that we-- the plan is
one that is consciously intended

41:44.436 --> 41:49.846 align:middle
to avoid creating market
strains and to allow the market

41:49.846 --> 41:53.916 align:middle
to adjust to a very gradual
and predictable plan.

41:54.446 --> 42:00.346 align:middle
My hope and expectation is that
when we decide to go forward

42:00.346 --> 42:05.066 align:middle
with this plan, that there will
be very little reaction to it,

42:05.326 --> 42:08.116 align:middle
that it's clear how
we intend to proceed,

42:08.506 --> 42:13.696 align:middle
and that this is something
that will just run quietly

42:13.696 --> 42:18.706 align:middle
in the background over a number
of years, leading to a reduction

42:18.706 --> 42:25.156 align:middle
in the size of our balance sheet
and in the outstanding stock

42:25.156 --> 42:29.076 align:middle
of reserves, and
that it's something

42:29.076 --> 42:34.066 align:middle
that the Committee will not be
reconsidering from time to time.

42:34.066 --> 42:37.076 align:middle
We think this is a
workable plan, and it will,

42:37.076 --> 42:41.146 align:middle
as one of my colleagues,
President Harker, described it,

42:41.406 --> 42:43.616 align:middle
it will be like watching
paint dry,

42:44.126 --> 42:46.656 align:middle
that this will just be something

42:46.656 --> 42:48.766 align:middle
that runs quietly
in the background.

42:48.976 --> 42:52.416 align:middle
So that's my expectation
and our intention.

42:52.986 --> 42:56.946 align:middle
Look, of course, if it turns
out that there is a surprise

42:56.946 --> 43:00.826 align:middle
and a substantial reaction,
that is something we would have

43:00.886 --> 43:03.026 align:middle
to take into account in deciding

43:03.026 --> 43:04.926 align:middle
on the appropriate
stance of policy.

43:04.926 --> 43:09.076 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

43:09.076 --> 43:11.106 align:middle
Michael McKee from Bloomberg
Television and Radio.

43:11.106 --> 43:12.176 align:middle
A couple of comments

43:12.176 --> 43:14.976 align:middle
from Administration officials I
wanted to get your reaction to.

43:15.296 --> 43:17.716 align:middle
The Treasury Secretary
and others have suggested

43:17.716 --> 43:20.866 align:middle
that Dodd-Frank regulation and
the way it's been implied--

43:20.866 --> 43:23.596 align:middle
applied have restricted
credit growth in the economy,

43:23.676 --> 43:26.536 align:middle
banks are not making loans
that they otherwise would,

43:26.816 --> 43:28.246 align:middle
and that has slowed growth.

43:28.606 --> 43:29.826 align:middle
Do you agree with that?

43:29.946 --> 43:32.576 align:middle
And if you get a new Vice
Chair of Supervision who wanted

43:32.576 --> 43:35.896 align:middle
to ease up on regulation,
would you go along with that?

43:36.326 --> 43:37.486 align:middle
Also, I wanted to ask you--

43:37.486 --> 43:40.956 align:middle
it's been reported the President
has congratulated you on being a

43:40.956 --> 43:42.386 align:middle
"low interest" person.

43:42.786 --> 43:44.816 align:middle
Would you agree with
that characterization

43:44.896 --> 43:45.916 align:middle
of your philosophy?

43:47.056 --> 43:48.056 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

43:48.826 --> 43:52.576 align:middle
Well, with respect to the
impact of credit conditions

43:52.576 --> 43:57.096 align:middle
and bank regulation on slow
growth, I've previously,

43:57.566 --> 44:03.746 align:middle
in testifying, indicated
that I don't think

44:04.126 --> 44:08.656 align:middle
that our regulations have
played an important role,

44:08.836 --> 44:13.256 align:middle
at least broadly speaking,
in impeding credit growth

44:13.626 --> 44:15.436 align:middle
and the growth of the economy.

44:15.726 --> 44:17.896 align:middle
And I've pointed in
the past to a number

44:17.896 --> 44:22.176 align:middle
of statistics suggesting
that credit growth continues

44:22.176 --> 44:27.186 align:middle
to be healthy, including
among small--

44:27.226 --> 44:31.006 align:middle
smaller community banks
that are most concerned

44:31.006 --> 44:32.496 align:middle
with regulatory burden.

44:32.866 --> 44:37.706 align:middle
I think when banks are
undercapitalized and weak,

44:37.976 --> 44:39.866 align:middle
that impedes credit growth.

44:40.366 --> 44:42.276 align:middle
And when banks are strong,

44:42.276 --> 44:44.626 align:middle
they're in a much
better position to lend.

44:46.126 --> 44:51.446 align:middle
So that's been a view that
I've stated previously.

44:52.176 --> 44:57.586 align:middle
Now, in terms of regulation,
the Treasury recently issued,

44:57.586 --> 45:00.506 align:middle
on Monday, a detailed report.

45:00.916 --> 45:04.336 align:middle
And that's quite a
complicated document with lots

45:04.336 --> 45:06.406 align:middle
of recommendations in it.

45:06.686 --> 45:11.176 align:middle
I haven't had a chance to review
it thoroughly, so I don't want

45:11.176 --> 45:13.846 align:middle
to comment on too many details.

45:13.906 --> 45:20.206 align:middle
But I would say it underscores
the importance of capital,

45:20.206 --> 45:24.116 align:middle
liquidity, stress testing,
and resolution planning

45:24.586 --> 45:31.916 align:middle
in having a safe and sound
banking system, which are views

45:31.916 --> 45:35.446 align:middle
that I and my colleagues
have long espoused.

45:37.036 --> 45:40.626 align:middle
Regulatory burden, when
it's possible to ease it,

45:40.966 --> 45:45.476 align:middle
and a good deal of the
Treasury report is focused

45:45.476 --> 45:48.566 align:middle
on regulatory burden,
that's something

45:48.566 --> 45:52.066 align:middle
that all regulators
should be looking to do.

45:52.696 --> 45:55.456 align:middle
We strongly believe
in the importance

45:55.456 --> 45:59.816 align:middle
of tailoring our regulation
to the size and complexity

46:00.176 --> 46:03.186 align:middle
of institutions, of finding ways

46:03.476 --> 46:06.366 align:middle
to relieve burden
for community banks.

46:06.656 --> 46:12.026 align:middle
We have been focused and had a
number of initiatives already

46:12.026 --> 46:15.056 align:middle
in that direction, looking
for ways, for example,

46:15.056 --> 46:19.136 align:middle
to simplify capital requirements
for community banks.

46:19.516 --> 46:23.896 align:middle
And we'll continue in that
direction and, in that sense,

46:24.076 --> 46:28.616 align:middle
in those areas certainly
share the views expressed

46:28.616 --> 46:30.176 align:middle
in the Treasury report.

46:30.386 --> 46:32.916 align:middle
So there are a number
of areas and suggestions

46:33.176 --> 46:35.996 align:middle
where our thinking aligns well.

46:36.216 --> 46:39.386 align:middle
There is probably some
areas where we're likely

46:39.386 --> 46:42.736 align:middle
to have differences but quite--

46:42.736 --> 46:45.566 align:middle
you know, quite a number of
areas where we're, you know,

46:45.686 --> 46:50.896 align:middle
likely to be able to and
already are taking actions

46:51.286 --> 46:53.636 align:middle
that are consistent with
those recommendations.

46:54.116 --> 46:54.386 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

46:54.386 --> 46:56.626 align:middle
And the characterization of you
as a low interest rate person?

46:56.666 --> 46:56.966 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

46:57.836 --> 47:01.526 align:middle
Well, I have felt that it's been
appropriate for interest rates

47:01.526 --> 47:04.796 align:middle
to remain low for
a very long time.

47:05.166 --> 47:08.186 align:middle
We are in the process of,
as the economy strengthens,

47:08.456 --> 47:11.096 align:middle
normalizing interest rates.

47:11.156 --> 47:13.506 align:middle
But, certainly, we've
had a lot of years

47:14.056 --> 47:15.926 align:middle
in which interest
rates have been low.

47:15.926 --> 47:20.166 align:middle
I thought it was necessary to
support the economy at that time

47:20.456 --> 47:23.116 align:middle
and was strongly in
favor of those policies.

47:24.606 --> 47:28.066 align:middle
KAREN MRACEK.

47:28.556 --> 47:31.076 align:middle
Thank you, Karen Mracek with
Market News International.

47:31.486 --> 47:35.326 align:middle
Today you guys outlined the
details of the plan to--

47:35.846 --> 47:37.816 align:middle
when you begin to
end reinvestments.

47:37.816 --> 47:40.106 align:middle
But you stopped short
of saying exactly

47:40.336 --> 47:42.606 align:middle
when it would be implemented,
only saying "this year,"

47:43.126 --> 47:46.466 align:middle
and adding that that depended
on how the economy evolved.

47:46.906 --> 47:50.386 align:middle
Can you tell us why you decided
not to go ahead with it today?

47:50.386 --> 47:52.396 align:middle
Is there certain conditions
that you're looking for?

47:52.826 --> 47:56.356 align:middle
And then, as a second question,
do you think that the--

47:56.606 --> 47:59.376 align:middle
you could raise rates
and begin implementation

47:59.376 --> 48:00.616 align:middle
of the plan at the same time?

48:00.876 --> 48:01.166 align:middle
Thank you.

48:01.836 --> 48:03.266 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

48:03.586 --> 48:08.746 align:middle
So we have tried as
meticulously as we can

48:08.746 --> 48:13.406 align:middle
to provide advanced warning to
markets about how we would go

48:13.406 --> 48:18.226 align:middle
about doing this so that market
participants can prepare.

48:19.906 --> 48:24.866 align:middle
And today's announcement is
another step in that process.

48:25.166 --> 48:28.406 align:middle
So we certainly wanted
to get this information

48:28.406 --> 48:33.576 align:middle
out before we actually undertake
the beginning of this plan.

48:34.146 --> 48:36.246 align:middle
And, you know, as
the statement says,

48:36.246 --> 48:39.796 align:middle
we've made no definite decision
on the timing of the plan, but--

48:40.166 --> 48:43.426 align:middle
the timing of initiating
the plan.

48:43.736 --> 48:48.706 align:middle
But if the economy evolves in
line with our expectations,

48:48.706 --> 48:51.306 align:middle
which, you know, we will
be watching-- always are--

48:52.076 --> 48:55.176 align:middle
we could put this into
effect relatively soon.

48:55.176 --> 48:59.986 align:middle
You asked about whether or not
we would do that and raise rates

49:00.206 --> 49:03.636 align:middle
at the same meeting, and I would
say, we've made no decision

49:03.636 --> 49:07.916 align:middle
about that, and it really
hinges on the outlook

49:07.916 --> 49:09.696 align:middle
and our assessment
of conditions.

49:10.656 --> 49:13.326 align:middle
VICTORIA GUIDA.

49:15.446 --> 49:17.396 align:middle
Hi, Victoria Guida
with Politico.

49:17.676 --> 49:19.906 align:middle
You mentioned the Treasury
report, and I just wanted

49:21.216 --> 49:24.716 align:middle
to ask, how much weight do
you give those recommendations

49:24.716 --> 49:26.416 align:middle
in considering, you know,

49:26.556 --> 49:28.606 align:middle
potential regulatory
changes moving forward?

49:29.546 --> 49:31.236 align:middle
And also, more specifically,

49:31.326 --> 49:33.836 align:middle
one of the recommendations
relates to the Volcker rule.

49:33.836 --> 49:35.936 align:middle
I know you've said in
the past that, you know,

49:35.936 --> 49:38.446 align:middle
it might be a good idea
to reduce the burden

49:38.966 --> 49:40.826 align:middle
on community banks,
but it also talks

49:40.826 --> 49:44.336 align:middle
about potentially exempting
banks with trading assets

49:44.336 --> 49:46.066 align:middle
and liabilities above
a certain level.

49:46.416 --> 49:47.846 align:middle
And I was wondering if you think

49:47.846 --> 49:50.936 align:middle
that that's an idea
that's worth exploring,

49:50.936 --> 49:51.666 align:middle
that might be a good idea.

49:53.056 --> 49:53.736 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

49:53.846 --> 49:59.736 align:middle
So we have previously suggested
exempting community banks,

49:59.736 --> 50:02.646 align:middle
smaller banks, entirely
from the rule.

50:02.646 --> 50:07.206 align:middle
And a number of my
colleagues have spoken

50:07.206 --> 50:09.926 align:middle
about the Volcker rule.

50:11.376 --> 50:19.856 align:middle
Implementation of it is frankly
complex, and I'm certainly open

50:19.856 --> 50:28.846 align:middle
to looking at ways to reduce
regulatory burden in that area.

50:29.356 --> 50:35.066 align:middle
The report endorses
a restriction

50:35.066 --> 50:36.826 align:middle
on proprietary trading.

50:37.436 --> 50:41.756 align:middle
So, in that sense, it
endorses the main objective

50:41.756 --> 50:45.316 align:middle
of the Volcker rule, which
I was pleased to see.

50:45.926 --> 50:50.126 align:middle
But on implementation, it's
true that the rules were--

50:50.426 --> 50:54.086 align:middle
in part reflecting the way
the legislation is written--

50:54.436 --> 50:57.126 align:middle
were, I think, necessarily
complex,

50:57.446 --> 51:02.496 align:middle
but we do have some ideas for
how we might simplify the rule,

51:02.776 --> 51:06.576 align:middle
and certainly it's something
we're quite open to looking at.

51:07.016 --> 51:08.156 align:middle
VICTORIA GUIDA.

51:08.376 --> 51:12.996 align:middle
And how much weight do you
give Treasury recommendations?

51:13.186 --> 51:13.766 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

51:13.766 --> 51:17.576 align:middle
Well, I mean, Treasury has a--
set out a list of objectives

51:17.646 --> 51:22.616 align:middle
for regulation that I'm
sympathetic to and endorse.

51:22.616 --> 51:27.826 align:middle
I think looking for ways to
reduce regulatory burden,

51:28.066 --> 51:32.676 align:middle
when it can be done without
sacrificing safety and soundness

51:33.056 --> 51:36.096 align:middle
or creating systemic
risk, that's something

51:36.446 --> 51:39.176 align:middle
that all regulators
should want to do.

51:39.176 --> 51:41.926 align:middle
We've done an awful
lot of rule writing

51:41.926 --> 51:44.336 align:middle
over the last five or six years.

51:44.666 --> 51:50.126 align:middle
And coming back and looking at
where we have created burdens

51:50.126 --> 51:53.476 align:middle
and ways in which we
can simplify to reduce,

51:53.756 --> 51:59.426 align:middle
that is an objective that is
core to the Treasury review

51:59.766 --> 52:02.966 align:middle
and that we are very
sympathetic with.

52:02.966 --> 52:05.866 align:middle
So, while we may not
agree on every detail,

52:06.106 --> 52:08.936 align:middle
certainly the suggestions
that are made there are ones

52:09.236 --> 52:13.346 align:middle
that we will pay attention to
and in many cases are already

52:13.346 --> 52:17.866 align:middle
on our own list of
things we should review.

52:19.116 --> 52:19.606 align:middle
MICHAEL DERBY.

52:22.166 --> 52:23.866 align:middle
Mike Derby from Dow
Jones Newswires.

52:23.866 --> 52:26.446 align:middle
In light of the plans to trim
the balance sheet hopefully

52:26.446 --> 52:28.996 align:middle
later this year, what
have you learned about QE

52:28.996 --> 52:33.156 align:middle
and your bond-buying policies
as a tool for monetary policy?

52:33.156 --> 52:34.796 align:middle
When they were launched, it
wasn't something you had,

52:35.106 --> 52:37.146 align:middle
you know, engaged in
on that scale before--

52:37.466 --> 52:40.086 align:middle
a lot of fears that it was
going to create hyperinflation.

52:40.086 --> 52:42.196 align:middle
That hasn't ever
seemed to come to pass.

52:43.006 --> 52:46.246 align:middle
So, in light of QE
as potentially a tool

52:46.246 --> 52:48.356 align:middle
for the future-- you know,
it might come back again--

52:48.686 --> 52:51.036 align:middle
what have you learned about
how it works in the economy?

52:51.036 --> 52:53.066 align:middle
Like, where do you
see it affect things?

52:53.066 --> 52:53.856 align:middle
You know, what are sort

52:53.856 --> 52:55.596 align:middle
of the lessons learned
of the experience?

52:55.766 --> 52:56.006 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

52:56.046 --> 52:56.426 align:middle
Well, thanks.

52:56.426 --> 52:58.026 align:middle
That's a great question.

52:58.476 --> 53:01.096 align:middle
I mean, staff in
the Federal Reserve

53:01.096 --> 53:04.976 align:middle
and outside economists
have done a great deal

53:04.976 --> 53:08.186 align:middle
of work trying to evaluate QE.

53:08.636 --> 53:13.256 align:middle
I think the general
conclusion is that it has worked

53:14.036 --> 53:16.606 align:middle
in that it has put
some downward pressure

53:16.606 --> 53:21.336 align:middle
on longer-term interest rates,
so-called term premiums embedded

53:21.336 --> 53:24.336 align:middle
in longer-term interest rates.

53:24.616 --> 53:27.246 align:middle
There's disagreement
among economists

53:27.246 --> 53:31.226 align:middle
about exactly how large
those effects are,

53:31.456 --> 53:34.386 align:middle
and it's something that's
difficult to pin down.

53:34.686 --> 53:39.436 align:middle
But, obviously, it has not
caused runaway inflation,

53:39.436 --> 53:40.646 align:middle
quite the contrary.

53:40.946 --> 53:44.536 align:middle
I mean, that was never my
expectation, but I do remember

53:44.756 --> 53:48.116 align:middle
when people were afraid
that that would happen.

53:48.296 --> 53:49.826 align:middle
We do have the tools.

53:49.826 --> 53:53.096 align:middle
We have, you know, even
with a large balance sheet--

53:53.096 --> 53:55.576 align:middle
we intend to shrink
our balance sheet now,

53:55.576 --> 53:59.726 align:middle
but even with a large balance
sheet, we retain the ability

54:00.066 --> 54:04.506 align:middle
to move the fed funds rate
and set it as appropriate

54:04.506 --> 54:06.386 align:middle
for the needs of the economy.

54:06.726 --> 54:11.146 align:middle
So I think we have
learned that it works.

54:11.416 --> 54:13.816 align:middle
It's a valuable part
of the toolkit.

54:14.256 --> 54:18.806 align:middle
It's something that, if we
were to encounter an episode

54:18.996 --> 54:22.226 align:middle
in the future of
extreme weakness where--

54:22.526 --> 54:25.846 align:middle
I've said we want the fed
funds rate and movements

54:25.846 --> 54:27.446 align:middle
in short-term interest rates,

54:27.446 --> 54:34.426 align:middle
that's our go-to number
one main policy tool--

54:34.686 --> 54:39.386 align:middle
but if we were to hit the zero
lower bound and constrained

54:39.386 --> 54:43.516 align:middle
in our use of that tool,
certainly balance sheet policies

54:43.516 --> 54:47.966 align:middle
and forward guidance of the type
that we provided, I believe,

54:48.076 --> 54:50.146 align:middle
based on the evidence
of how they worked,

54:50.146 --> 54:52.496 align:middle
ought to remain part
of our toolkit.

54:52.816 --> 54:57.026 align:middle
And we have said in the
bullets that we released today

54:57.026 --> 54:59.906 align:middle
on our balance sheet
that in such of--

55:00.066 --> 55:03.366 align:middle
an episode of such extreme
weakness in the future,

55:03.576 --> 55:05.916 align:middle
those are things we would
consider going forward.

55:05.916 --> 55:06.426 align:middle
MICHAEL DERBY.

55:06.426 --> 55:07.286 align:middle
One small follow-up.

55:07.286 --> 55:11.036 align:middle
Is $4-1/2 trillion
sort of a natural limit

55:11.036 --> 55:13.216 align:middle
to how high you might want
to push the balance sheet,

55:13.216 --> 55:15.406 align:middle
or could you envision it going
higher if you needed it to?

55:15.486 --> 55:15.766 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

55:16.366 --> 55:20.026 align:middle
Well, we've had no discussion
of that issue, you know.

55:20.356 --> 55:24.856 align:middle
And our focus now is on getting
it back to a more normal size.

55:25.256 --> 55:30.186 align:middle
But I would say, the use of QE
in the United States relative

55:30.246 --> 55:34.036 align:middle
to the size of our economy
is not as high as it's been

55:34.036 --> 55:36.756 align:middle
in some other countries
that have employed it.

55:37.026 --> 55:41.176 align:middle
But that's something we haven't
seriously even discussed.

55:42.026 --> 55:43.256 align:middle
PATRICK GILLESPIE.

55:45.996 --> 55:48.266 align:middle
Chair Yellen, Patrick
Gillespie with CNN.

55:48.466 --> 55:50.476 align:middle
It's workforce development
week at the White House,

55:50.476 --> 55:53.016 align:middle
and you highlighted
several job training--

55:53.126 --> 55:55.426 align:middle
successful job training programs
in your speech in March.

55:55.926 --> 55:58.216 align:middle
The President's budget
has a 40 percent cut

55:58.316 --> 55:59.406 align:middle
to job training programs.

55:59.796 --> 56:02.066 align:middle
What is your response
to the budget cut

56:02.066 --> 56:03.766 align:middle
to job training programs
even though they expect

56:03.766 --> 56:06.196 align:middle
to expand apprenticeship
programs?

56:06.496 --> 56:08.186 align:middle
And do you believe that
job training programs

56:08.216 --> 56:09.686 align:middle
and apprenticeships are needed

56:09.686 --> 56:11.796 align:middle
to help fix the job
skills gap in the economy?

56:11.796 --> 56:12.186 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

56:12.946 --> 56:15.686 align:middle
Well, I'm not going to comment
on the President's budget,

56:16.126 --> 56:19.706 align:middle
and these programs
can be undertaken

56:19.706 --> 56:21.066 align:middle
at many different levels.

56:21.066 --> 56:29.756 align:middle
I've seen many nonprofits and
states and local authorities put

56:29.756 --> 56:34.816 align:middle
in place programs that looked
to me to be successful.

56:35.306 --> 56:40.746 align:middle
I do think we have a
tight labor market and one

56:40.906 --> 56:43.686 align:middle
where employers have jobs

56:43.686 --> 56:48.206 align:middle
where they're finding
difficulty identifying workers

56:48.206 --> 56:49.906 align:middle
with the appropriate skills.

56:50.286 --> 56:53.226 align:middle
In my own discussions
with businesses,

56:53.456 --> 56:57.006 align:middle
what I hear more of-- and this
is something I think is a great

56:57.006 --> 57:01.716 align:middle
aspect of a tight labor market--
larger firms are spending more

57:01.716 --> 57:07.766 align:middle
on training and trying to, given
that they can't hire workers

57:07.766 --> 57:14.956 align:middle
with the ideal skills, are
training people to fill jobs

57:15.156 --> 57:17.016 align:middle
that they have available.

57:17.436 --> 57:21.746 align:middle
What I hear from CEOs
of smaller firms is

57:21.836 --> 57:25.926 align:middle
that they don't have the
ability to launch such programs.

57:26.166 --> 57:31.276 align:middle
They would really very much
like to participate along,

57:31.456 --> 57:36.606 align:middle
for example, with community
colleges or nonprofits to see

57:36.606 --> 57:41.566 align:middle
such programs launched and have
jobs that people could fill

57:41.566 --> 57:44.006 align:middle
if they received the
appropriate training.

57:44.356 --> 57:48.736 align:middle
So I do think that this
is an important area, and,

57:49.036 --> 57:53.516 align:middle
especially given the
pressures that have, you know,

57:53.516 --> 57:57.366 align:middle
existed for a long time--
downward pressure on wages

57:57.626 --> 58:00.516 align:middle
and job opportunities,
especially for those

58:00.516 --> 58:03.776 align:middle
with less skills-- I think
that this is something

58:03.776 --> 58:05.166 align:middle
that deserves priority.

